From: Artem Bambalov <artembambalov1993@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] c2allocatorgralloc: wait for the fence unlock hands back

unlock does not finish the cache maintenance an allocator owes a buffer
the CPU has written -- it starts it, and hands back a fence that says
when the writes have landed. This dropped that fence on the floor, and
filled in none of the C2Fence the caller offered to carry it in; both
spots said TODO.

Nothing else in the pipeline waits either, so nothing ever made those
writes land in time. Whoever took the buffer next -- in another process,
through its own mapping -- read it while the writes were still in the
writer's caches.

What that looked like here: video thumbnails torn. A 2560x1440 clip
decoded by the software AVC decoder came back with about a quarter of
the picture holding whatever the block had held before it was recycled,
a different quarter on every run, while the same decoder was word for
word correct at 1280x720 and below. The bigger the frame the wider the
window, which is why only the two largest clips on hand were ever wrong.

It took a while to get here because almost everything else looked
guilty first. Measured and ruled out: the pitch the decoder writes with
(it matches the block's own on every size), the copy out of the decoder
(a plain row-by-row memcpy), the retriever's path (the source buffer is
exactly stride*height*3/2 and the crop is the whole picture), the
block pool (holding blocks back changes nothing), the level and DPB,
input truncation, and the decoder's thread count. What finally pointed
here was that a large read of the block in the producer made the frame
whole -- and a delay of the same length did not. Reading evicts the
dirty lines by volume; waiting does not evict anything.

With the fence waited on, the eighteen test clips decode to the same
checksums run after run, and the two that were torn are clean.

This is our tree's own fix: the TODO is still there in every AOSP
release through 14, and C2Fence has no implementation on this path in
any of them.

Signed-off-by: Artem Bambalov <artembambalov1993@gmail.com>

---
diff --git a/media/codec2/vndk/C2AllocatorGralloc.cpp b/media/codec2/vndk/C2AllocatorGralloc.cpp
index af97e61fc..bd3ee5113 100644
--- a/media/codec2/vndk/C2AllocatorGralloc.cpp
+++ b/media/codec2/vndk/C2AllocatorGralloc.cpp
@@ -24,6 +24,7 @@
 #include <android/hardware/graphics/mapper/3.0/IMapper.h>
 #include <cutils/native_handle.h>
 #include <hardware/gralloc.h>
+#include <ui/Fence.h>
 
 #include <C2AllocatorGralloc.h>
 #include <C2Buffer.h>
@@ -818,43 +819,72 @@ c2_status_t C2AllocationGralloc::map(
 
 c2_status_t C2AllocationGralloc::unmap(
         uint8_t **addr, C2Rect rect, C2Fence *fence /* nullable */) {
-    // TODO: check addr and size, use fence
+    // TODO: check addr and size
     (void)addr;
     (void)rect;
+    /* The caller asks for a fence it could wait on later; this one waits here
+     * instead, so there is nothing left to hand back. */
+    (void)fence;
 
     std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mMappedLock);
     c2_status_t err = C2_OK;
+
+    /*
+     * unlock only starts the cache maintenance the allocator owes a buffer the
+     * CPU has written; the fence it hands back is what says those writes have
+     * landed. Dropping it, which is what the TODO here used to do, lets
+     * whoever takes the buffer next read it while the writes are still in this
+     * core's caches -- and since nothing else in the pipeline waits either,
+     * nothing ever makes them land in time.
+     *
+     * Measured on this board: a 2560x1440 clip decoded by the software AVC
+     * decoder came back with a quarter of the picture holding whatever the
+     * block held before, differently on every run, while the same decoder was
+     * word for word correct at 1280x720. The frame came out whole only when
+     * something in the producer happened to read enough of the block to evict
+     * those lines by volume -- a delay of the same length did nothing, and
+     * neither did zeroing the block, holding it out of the pool, asking the
+     * allocator for a cacheable one, or reordering the unlock against the
+     * hand-off. Waiting here is what fixes it, and it fixes it every time:
+     * the same eighteen frames now check out identical run after run.
+     *
+     * The fence is closed by Fence's destructor, which takes the fd.
+     */
+    int releaseFenceFd = -1;
+    auto collect = [&err, &releaseFenceFd](
+            const auto &maperr, const auto &releaseFence) {
+        err = maperr2error(maperr);
+        if (err != C2_OK) {
+            return;
+        }
+        const native_handle_t *h = releaseFence.getNativeHandle();
+        if (h != nullptr && h->numFds == 1 && h->data[0] >= 0) {
+            releaseFenceFd = ::dup(h->data[0]);
+        }
+    };
+
     if (mMapper2) {
         if (!mMapper2->unlock(
-                const_cast<native_handle_t *>(mBuffer),
-                [&err, &fence](const auto &maperr, const auto &releaseFence) {
-                    // TODO
-                    (void) fence;
-                    (void) releaseFence;
-                    err = maperr2error(maperr);
-                    if (err == C2_OK) {
-                        // TODO: fence
-                    }
-                }).isOk()) {
+                const_cast<native_handle_t *>(mBuffer), collect).isOk()) {
             ALOGE("failed transaction: unlock");
             return C2_CORRUPTED;
         }
     } else {
         if (!mMapper3->unlock(
-                const_cast<native_handle_t *>(mBuffer),
-                [&err, &fence](const auto &maperr, const auto &releaseFence) {
-                    // TODO
-                    (void) fence;
-                    (void) releaseFence;
-                    err = maperr2error(maperr);
-                    if (err == C2_OK) {
-                        // TODO: fence
-                    }
-                }).isOk()) {
+                const_cast<native_handle_t *>(mBuffer), collect).isOk()) {
             ALOGE("failed transaction: unlock (@3.0)");
             return C2_CORRUPTED;
         }
     }
+
+    if (releaseFenceFd >= 0) {
+        static constexpr int kReleaseWaitMs = 1000;
+        sp<Fence> released = new Fence(releaseFenceFd);
+        if (released->wait(kReleaseWaitMs) != NO_ERROR) {
+            ALOGW("release fence did not signal in %dms", kReleaseWaitMs);
+        }
+    }
+
     if (err == C2_OK) {
         mLocked = false;
     }
